    thin+tau150   drift   39.2   range   39.8   (JAW-GATED, same anchor sweep)
    v66-thin      drift   87.6   range   38.5   (JAW-GATED, same anchor sweep)
    ungated drift span [46.5, 55.1]   gated [29.0, 87.6]
    at the overlap midpoint, drift 50.8 Hz:  UNGATED range 60.8, JAW-GATED 39.5
    the jaw's phase is worth -35.1% of per-cycle range at matched drift
    THE JAW IS THE WORSE SCHEDULE. At matched drift the anchor scheduled by
    the creature's OWN silences carries MORE per-cycle excursion than the
    jaw-scheduled one. The pre-registered claim is not merely unsupported,
    it comes out backwards -- so `the jaw cycle is a boundary for F1` is
    REFUSED, and what the jaw buys is a LOWER floor on drift (it reaches
    29.0 Hz where the ungated schedule saturates at 46.5) rather than any
    gain in articulation. Two trade-off points on two curves, neither
    dominating, and the frame reading has no measurement behind it.

  --- the gate or the anchor? ---
    v62-nogate    (tau 633, no gate)  drift  137.5
    nogate+tau60  (tau  60, no gate)  drift   55.1
    thin+tau60    (tau  60, GATED)    drift   29.0
    the short anchor alone accounts for 76% of the bounding
    REPORTED AS A QUANTITY, NOT A LABEL: the short anchor alone accounts for
    76% of the bounding, so most of what v66 achieves is available with no
    gate at all -- `f1_return_tau_ms` at 633 ms was simply too long. Whether
    the remainder is the JAW is the deciding test above, not this split.

